At a first glance, it is not clear if the different areas of learning including supervised learning, unsupervised learning, self-supervised learning, and reinforcement learning have any useful language in common. Naiivly, one might say mathematics is the only common language between that mathematics is the universal language of learning; however, this statement is vague, and more importantly not useful. This is similar to saying that every problem is an optimization problem. For a language of learning to be useful, one must be able use to to develop good theories of learning.

In this post, I'm not trying to argue against mathematics; rather, I intent to make the argument that, probability theory, a subfield of mathematics, is more suited and in fact more natural be describing learning machines and developing powerful theories of learning. In other words, probability theory is the universal language of supervised learning, unsupervised learning, self-supervised learning, and reinforcement learning. In fact, it is not overexcaturation to say, all of learning can be reduced to probability density estimation. A core problem in statistical inference.

Now, this shouldn't imply that, by casting the different types of learning in the language of probability theory, one should expect an immediate solution to the learning problem. Rather, I argue that, probability theory provide us with a universal language and a desciplined approach for building learning machines. In addition, when combined with statistical inference, the different issues faced in practice are clarified. And get us closer to universal laws of learning.

Before we could dive into the details of the argument, a quick note regarding artificial neural networks and deep learning must be made. Deep learning, the practice of minimizing some loss functional over a collection of functions modelled using a multi-layered network of nodes is a mere method. In fact, it is a subfield of the calculus of variations. Deep learning is more similar to the method of least squares that it is to the theory of gravity. One could use the method of least squares to estimate the gravitational constant given a model of motion, and some observations of falling objects; but, no matter how hard you try, it does not provide a useful langauge capabable of describing or deriving newton's law of motion, the theory of general relativity, or a universal theory of physics.

In the rest of this post, we start by recalling the universal language of supervised learning while we emphasize a statistical inference approach rather than the classifical optimization approach developed by Vapnik. We pay a lot of attention to the notion of statistical "manifolds", the likelihood principale, divergence, bayesian inference, posterior density estimation, evidence, and model selection. We then extend these ideas to problems in supervised learning and self supervised learning by introducing the notion of latent variables and showing that the language is indeed universal. We close by appyling extending the language to describe problems in reinforcement learning by introducing the notion of time, conditional independence, markov chains, non-linear filtering and stochastic control.

This article assumes some mathematical maturity and good knowledge of calculus based probability theory (probability spaces; random variables; joint, marginal and conditional probability functions; and expectation), statistical inference in regular statistical models (parameters, bayes rule, likelihood principal, sufficient statistics, unbiased and consistent estimators), and some familiarity with machine learning problems (supervised learning, unsupervised learning, and reinforcement learning). A good understanding of set theory, linear algebra, groups, and smooth manifolds is preferred but not necessary.

<h3>Statistical inference and supervised learning</h3>

The use of probability theory to characterize supervised learning problems have been rigorously introduced by Vapnik. As is typically the case, we are given a finite set of observations from two observed quantities, and asked to predict future values of one, given observed the other. 

 For instance, one can cast problems in supervised learning as that of choosing a function that minimizes some objective from a collection of functions that relate some input quantity to another output quantity. In addition, one could cast problems in self supervised learning as that of choosing an operator that minimizes the construction loss from a collection of operators on the space of some input quantity.

 Despite the correctness of the previous statement, it is not of practical use. To make it practical one must be able to say, what are the building blocks of a model, how different a collection of models different from others, and more importantly, given some data, how should one choose one model over another. 


All learning problems including classification and regression in supervised learning, unsupervised learning, self supervised learning, and reinforcement learning are naturally probabilistic. As a result, statistical learning theory is the natural mathematical theory for studying learning machines. This fact is known to researchers in the fields of machine learning, statistical inference, and engineering. However, it is often the case that new comers to these fields are often unaware of statistical learning, and more importantly to the fact that, all learning algorithms are algorithms for estimating an unknown probability distribution from samples.

It is typical in computer science that learning problems are classified into different classes such as supervised learning, unsupervised learning, self-supervised learning, and reinforcement learning. Even more, problems in supervised learning are typically further subclassified into classification and regression problems. It is true that certain learning algorithms are more useful in a particular class of learning problems; however, all these learning algorithms attempt to solve one problem, the problem of estimating a probability density/mass function from data. 

Probability theory and statistical estimation provides a unified framework for all learning problems. This includes regression and classification in supervised learning, unsupervised learning problems, self-supervised learning problems and even that of reinforcement learning. This fact is known for all researchers in the field but not as clear to new comers in machine learning, statistical inference, signal processing, and adaptive control. To beging, we will introduce the problem of density estimation, 
Supervised learning is concerned with the estimation of the joint probability measure of two random variables. In this method of learning from observations we are typically interested in predicting the values of one or more observable quantities $y_i, i\in \{1,\cdots, K\}$ using the values of other observable quantities $x_j,j\in \{1,\cdots, N\}$ {% cite friedman:an-overview-of-predictive-learning-and-fn-approx:1994 %}. This general case includes both classification (when the values of each $y_i$ are finite) and regression (the case where the range of values of each $y_i$ and $x_j$ is infinite). To keep things simple, we will assume that $K=N=1$. This simplification does not change the formality of the learning problem because we can always consider multi-valued random variables instead of single-valued random variables.

As a concrete example, consider the problem of building a learning machine to classify, using previous observations as samples, whether or not a $10\times 10$ grayscale image is for a handwritten number $4$ on an envelop or as a graffiti on a wall. In this case, $x$ represents the physical apperance of a handwritten number which we could capture with a camera at anytime, while $y$ is an indicator of the presence of the number $4$.

In order to solve the problem, we need to assign numbers and a scale to our measurements of the observable quantities $x, y$. For $y$, a typical convention is to assign the number $1$ if the image contains the number $4$ and $0$ otherwise. Keep in mind that there is nothing special about the numbers $0,1$ other than that this notation simplifies our mathematical expressions a little. For $x$, a reasonable choice is for each pixel to vary in the interval $[0,1]$ where $0$ represents the color white, and $1$ the color black. Mathematically, this is a reasonable choice because the intensity of color is linked to the physical notion of electromagnetic frequency which is an infinite quantity. As was the case for $y$, the interval $[0,1]$ is arbitrary and if our model is to be successful (with respect to its ability to generalize), our predictions should not be impacted if we change $[0,1]$ to say $[-10,10]$. These invariances of scale are important and we will have more to say about them in a future post.

<h2>The learning problem is probabilistic in nature</h2>
There are two primary reasons any learning machine should treat the observed quantities as random. The first is a result of the fact that all measurements are corrupted by noise. In the example above, noise enter our measurements of $x$ due to factors such the variability of the manufactoruing process of cameras and/or the lighting conditions at the time of capturing images. For $y$, noise could be a result of having different observers each of whom apply different mental rules for judging when a handwritten digit is $9$ rather than a $4$.

The other reason is due to the complexity of the physical world. In the real world, any one phenomena is typically a result of the interaction of many other quantities. For the relationship between $x$ and $y$ to be deterministic, once must observe all the quantities that cause variations in the values of $y$. For learning machines, complete knowledge of all the quantities that can deterministically explain the variations in $y$ can be very impractical and in some cases impossible.

As a toy example, consider the case where one attempts to build a learning machine to predict the total distance a rocket at rest travels given knowledge of the initial force applied to launch the rocket, the launch angle, and the total mass of the rocket. Suppose that observers collected the training data using a somewhat precise measureing device without measuring the viscosity and temperature of the surrounding air. In this case, the true relationship between the predictor quantities (initial force, launch angle, rocket mass) and that of the total distance travelled by the rocket is not deterministic. In fact, the relationship is mostly likely not deterministic without taking into account quantum mechanical effects whose effects could be measured if one has a very precise distance measuring device. As a result, it is impractical to measure all quantities that can cause variation in the total distance travelled by the rocket; rather, one should to using some quantities that can reasonably predict the total distance travelled by the rocket as long as our predictions are useful.

 A possible immediate consequence is that for the same values of the projectile's initial velocity and mass different displacements of the projectiles will be measured not due to noise but because we did not take into account the viscosity of the air. One should conclude that observing all quantities that influence some quantity of interest is very unlikely to come by without painstaking scientific investigation, and experimental design. As a result, any learning machine operating in the real world should assume that values of $x$ does not uniquely determine values of $y$.

<h3>The supervised learning problem</h3>
In the language of probability theory, one can describe the variations in our observations of $x$ and $y$ by two random variables $X$ and $Y$ with values in the sets $\mathcal{X}:=\{\text{all }10\times 10\text{ grayscale images}\}$ and $\mathcal{Y}:=\{0,1\}$ respectively. To rigorously define $X$ and $Y$ as random variables, one should first define the probability space on which they are to be defined. In the machine learning liteature this is often ignored even though for new students of machine learning, this could cause unnecessary confusion. To see that, suppose a new camera technology was developed such that our images are now $100\times 100$ pixels. The physical objects we are taking pictures of have not changed as a result of changing the camera. In other words, one should not confuse the environment in which the number $4$ physically exists with the images generated by some measuring device.

Defining the probability space on the environment is rather hard and complex. To make progress, we will assume that our mathematical description of the physical environment admits a probability space $(\Omega, \mathcal{B}(\Omega), \mathbb{P})$, where $\Omega$ is the set of all outcomes, $\mathcal{B}(\Omega)$ is the relevant Sigma algebra, and $\mathbb{P}$ is some probability measure. Formally, the random variables are defined as the measurable functions $X:\Omega\rightarrow\mathcal{X}\subset\mathbb{R}^{10\times 10}$, and $Y:\Omega\rightarrow\mathcal{Y}\subset\mathbb{R}$.

Now suppose that the quantities $x$ and $y$ are linked in the real word by some deterministic function $g$ such that 

$$\tag{1}
y=g(x,z)
$$

where $z$ is an unobserved and non-controlled quantity. To account for the measurements noise of $y,x$ and uncertainity about $z$, we modify the model by adding a stochastic term $e$, leadings us to the general statistical model 
$$\tag{2}
Y=f(X,e)
$$
where $f$ is a deterministic measurable map and $e$ is a random variable with values in an unknown set $E$ (also defined on the probability space $\Omega$). It should be clear that solving this problem requires at minimum making some assumption about the nature of $e$. Even more, if the set of all measurable maps $\mathcal{F}:=\mathcal{X}\times E\rightarrow\mathcal{Y}$ is infinite, in general we have an ill-posed problem. This is a result of the fact that our data is always finite and we are trying to select an element from an infinite dimensional space $\mathcal{F}$.

Statistical learning theory and mathematical statistics are concerned with approximating the function $f$ by choosing an element of $\mathcal{F}$ after making some assumptions about the nature of $f$ and $e$. In statistical learning theory, these assumptions are typically reflected in the choice of some model space (a well behaving subset of $\mathcal{F}$) and a loss function which further refines the model space and establishes a notion of distance between its elements.

There is another way of looking at the statistical learning problem above using the language of information geometry. Regardless of the nature of $f$ and $e$, there exist some joint probability measure on the product set $\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{Y}$ that represents the true probabilistic relationship between $X$ and $Y$. That data generating process (DGP) is thought of as an element in the set $\mathcal{M}$ of all probability measures on $\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{Y}$. Solving the statistical learning problem amounts to choosing an element from $\mathcal{M}$ that best explains the data collected about $X,Y$. 

It is important to note, this reformulation of the problem, does not necessarily solves the ill-posedness of the general learning problem. To see that, note if the set $\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{Y}$ is infinite, which is the case in our running example, the set of all probability measures on it is infinite. It is well known that the general approximating of a probability density on an infinite set using finite observations is an ill-posed problem (except for some special cases such as guassian family of probability distributions).

